31
00:00:52,484 --> 00:00:55,005
a year or two of retirement income,

32
00:00:55,005 --> 00:00:56,878
or a debt free start for a

33
00:00:56,878 --> 00:00:57,310
child.

34
00:00:57,490 --> 00:00:59,512
And it came from money most people

35
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:00,880
spend without noticing.

36
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:03,594
More importantly, one hundred dollars a month

37
00:01:03,594 --> 00:01:04,833
is just the beginning.

38
00:01:05,013 --> 00:01:06,850
Once you see how the math works,

39
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:09,377
you'll understand why people who start small

40
00:01:09,377 --> 00:01:11,271
often end up investing much more.

41
00:01:11,621 --> 00:01:14,245
We'll explore this from three angles.

42
00:01:14,425 --> 00:01:15,923
First, the numbers.

43
00:01:16,103 --> 00:01:18,639
What one hundred dollars a month becomes

44
00:01:18,639 --> 00:01:19,970
at different returns,

45
00:01:19,970 --> 00:01:21,744
over different time periods,

46
00:01:21,744 --> 00:01:22,695
and after fees,

47
00:01:22,695 --> 00:01:23,963
taxes and inflation.

48
00:01:24,143 --> 00:01:26,233
Second, the psychology.

49
00:01:26,413 --> 00:01:28,674
Why small amounts feel pointless,

50
00:01:28,674 --> 00:01:31,277
and why that feeling is so misleading.

51
00:01:31,457 --> 00:01:33,025
And third, the playbook.

52
00:01:33,205 --> 00:01:35,413
Exactly where to put your one hundred

53
00:01:35,413 --> 00:01:36,786
dollars, in what order,

54
00:01:36,786 --> 00:01:38,696
so it works as hard as possible.

55
00:01:38,876 --> 00:01:39,828
Quick reminder.

56
00:01:40,008 --> 00:01:42,503
Seven percent is an illustration,

57
00:01:42,503 --> 00:01:43,561
not a promise.

58
00:01:43,741 --> 00:01:45,380
Markets go up and down,

59
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:47,305
and real returns will vary.

60
00:01:47,656 --> 00:01:49,281
Let's start with the numbers.

61
00:01:49,461 --> 00:01:51,329
And the first lesson is about the

62
00:01:51,329 --> 00:01:53,989
most important ingredient in this whole recipe.

63
00:01:54,339 --> 00:01:55,128
Our numbers.

64
00:01:55,308 --> 00:01:58,762
All growth examples use monthly compounding with

65
00:01:58,762 --> 00:02:00,921
stated average yearly returns,

66
00:02:00,921 --> 00:02:03,296
like four, seven and ten percent.

67
00:02:03,476 --> 00:02:05,064
These are illustrations,

68
00:02:05,064 --> 00:02:06,123
not predictions.

69
00:02:06,303 --> 00:02:09,474
Historical stock market data comes from sources

70
00:02:09,474 --> 00:02:11,228
like the S and P Dow Jones

71
00:02:11,228 --> 00:02:14,062
Indices, and long run return research from

72
00:02:14,062 --> 00:02:17,031
economists like Aswath Damodaran at New York

73
00:02:17,031 --> 00:02:17,773
University.

74
00:02:17,953 --> 00:02:20,496
Account rules come from the IRS.

75
00:02:20,676 --> 00:02:23,215
Inflation data comes from the Bureau of

76
00:02:23,215 --> 00:02:24,321
Labor Statistics.

77
00:02:24,501 --> 00:02:25,825
This is education,

78
00:02:25,825 --> 00:02:27,369
not financial advice.

79
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:28,416
Angle one.

80
00:02:28,596 --> 00:02:29,304
The numbers.

81
00:02:29,484 --> 00:02:31,978
Seven lessons about what one hundred dollars

82
00:02:31,978 --> 00:02:33,281
a month really becomes.

83
00:02:33,631 --> 00:02:34,652
Lesson number one.

84
00:02:34,832 --> 00:02:36,713
Time matters more than the amount.

85
00:02:37,063 --> 00:02:39,224
Here's what one hundred dollars a month

86
00:02:39,224 --> 00:02:41,496
becomes at a seven percent average yearly

87
00:02:41,496 --> 00:02:43,657
return, over different lengths of time.

88
00:02:43,837 --> 00:02:44,981
After ten years,

89
00:02:44,981 --> 00:02:47,483
roughly seventeen thousand dollars.

90
00:02:47,663 --> 00:02:48,957
After twenty years,

91
00:02:48,957 --> 00:02:50,797
roughly fifty two thousand.

92
00:02:50,977 --> 00:02:52,118
After thirty years,

93
00:02:52,118 --> 00:02:54,518
roughly one hundred twenty two thousand.

94
00:02:54,698 --> 00:02:55,813
After forty years,

95
00:02:55,813 --> 00:02:58,228
roughly two hundred sixty two thousand.

96
00:02:58,408 --> 00:02:59,511
Notice something.

97
00:02:59,691 --> 00:03:01,915
Each extra ten years more than doubles

98
00:03:01,915 --> 00:03:02,558
the result.

99
00:03:02,909 --> 00:03:03,756
That's compounding.

100
00:03:03,936 --> 00:03:05,005
In the early years,

101
00:03:05,005 --> 00:03:06,863
most of your balance is money you

102
00:03:06,863 --> 00:03:07,257
put in.

103
00:03:07,437 --> 00:03:08,619
In the later years,

104
00:03:08,619 --> 00:03:10,548
most of it is growth on growth.

105
00:03:10,728 --> 00:03:12,490
By year forty in our example,

106
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:15,405
you've contributed forty eight thousand dollars,

107
00:03:15,405 --> 00:03:17,288
and the growth is more than two

108
00:03:17,288 --> 00:03:18,321
hundred thousand.

109
00:03:18,501 --> 00:03:20,567
Time doesn't just add to the result.

110
00:03:20,747 --> 00:03:21,816
It multiplies it.

111
00:03:22,166 --> 00:03:24,159
This is why starting early is so

112
00:03:24,159 --> 00:03:24,720
powerful.

113
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:27,499
Someone who invests one hundred dollars a

114
00:03:27,499 --> 00:03:29,718
month from age twenty five to sixty

115
00:03:29,718 --> 00:03:31,684
five could end up with far more

116
00:03:31,684 --> 00:03:34,473
than someone who invests two hundred dollars

117
00:03:34,473 --> 00:03:36,502
a month from forty five to sixty

118
00:03:36,502 --> 00:03:39,038
five, even though the second person puts

119
00:03:39,038 --> 00:03:41,004
in the same total amount over a

120
00:03:41,004 --> 00:03:41,955
shorter period.

121
00:03:42,135 --> 00:03:43,667
Time does the heavy lifting.

122
00:03:44,018 --> 00:03:44,842
Try this.

123
00:03:45,022 --> 00:03:48,203
Open a free compound interest calculator online.

124
00:03:48,383 --> 00:03:50,604
Enter one hundred dollars a month,

125
00:03:50,604 --> 00:03:53,022
seven percent, and your own number of

126
00:03:53,022 --> 00:03:54,525
years until retirement.

127
00:03:54,705 --> 00:03:56,381
Look at how much of the final

128
00:03:56,381 --> 00:03:57,363
number is growth.

129
00:03:57,543 --> 00:03:59,464
That one screen can change how you

130
00:03:59,464 --> 00:04:00,875
think about every dollar.

131
00:04:01,226 --> 00:04:02,700
Here's the forty year version.

132
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:05,154
One hundred dollars a month for forty

133
00:04:05,154 --> 00:04:07,612
years, at a seven percent average yearly

134
00:04:07,612 --> 00:04:10,193
return, grows to roughly two hundred sixty

135
00:04:10,193 --> 00:04:11,483
two thousand dollars.

136
00:04:11,663 --> 00:04:12,555
Total put in,

137
00:04:12,555 --> 00:04:13,997
forty eight thousand.

138
00:04:14,347 --> 00:04:15,612
Lesson number two.

139
00:04:15,792 --> 00:04:17,963
The return rate makes a huge difference.

140
00:04:18,313 --> 00:04:20,855
Let's try different returns over twenty years.

141
00:04:21,035 --> 00:04:21,943
At four percent,

142
00:04:21,943 --> 00:04:24,383
one hundred dollars a month becomes roughly

143
00:04:24,383 --> 00:04:26,085
thirty seven thousand dollars.

144
00:04:26,265 --> 00:04:27,468
At seven percent,

145
00:04:27,468 --> 00:04:29,377
roughly fifty two thousand.

146
00:04:29,557 --> 00:04:30,756
At ten percent,

147
00:04:30,756 --> 00:04:33,075
roughly seventy six thousand.

148
00:04:33,255 --> 00:04:34,729
Same contributions.

149
00:04:34,909 --> 00:04:35,873
Same time.

150
00:04:36,053 --> 00:04:38,212
The only difference is the average return.

151
00:04:38,563 --> 00:04:40,072
Where do those numbers come from?

152
00:04:40,252 --> 00:04:41,978
Over very long periods,

153
00:04:41,978 --> 00:04:43,478
the US stock market,

154
00:04:43,478 --> 00:04:45,429
as measured by the S and P

155
00:04:45,429 --> 00:04:49,256
five hundred, has historically returned roughly ten

156
00:04:49,256 --> 00:04:52,483
percent a year on average before inflation,

157
00:04:52,483 --> 00:04:54,509
according to long run data.

158
00:04:54,689 --> 00:04:57,498
After inflation, it's been closer to seven

159
00:04:57,498 --> 00:04:58,033
percent.

160
00:04:58,213 --> 00:05:00,407
Bonds have historically returned less.

161
00:05:00,587 --> 00:05:03,513
A savings account usually returns less still.

162
00:05:03,693 --> 00:05:05,881
But past returns are no guarantee of

163
00:05:05,881 --> 00:05:08,434
future returns, and some decades have been

164
00:05:08,434 --> 00:05:09,892
much worse than average.

165
00:05:10,243 --> 00:05:12,521
That's why many people use seven percent

166
00:05:12,521 --> 00:05:13,261
for planning.

167
00:05:13,441 --> 00:05:15,736
It's a rough estimate of long run

168
00:05:15,736 --> 00:05:17,822
stock returns after inflation,

169
00:05:17,822 --> 00:05:20,326
so the result is in today's dollars.

170
00:05:20,506 --> 00:05:21,796
If you use ten percent,

171
00:05:21,796 --> 00:05:23,929
you should remember that the result is

172
00:05:23,929 --> 00:05:24,939
in future dollars,

173
00:05:24,939 --> 00:05:27,240
which will buy less because of inflation.

174
00:05:27,590 --> 00:05:28,449
Try this.

175
00:05:28,629 --> 00:05:30,611
Run your numbers at four,

176
00:05:30,611 --> 00:05:32,355
seven and ten percent.

177
00:05:32,535 --> 00:05:34,823
Plan with the middle or lower number,

178
00:05:34,823 --> 00:05:37,110
and treat anything better as a bonus.

179
00:05:37,290 --> 00:05:41,040
Conservative planning means fewer unpleasant surprises.

180
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:42,609
Lesson number three.

181
00:05:42,789 --> 00:05:44,449
Fees matter more than you think.

182
00:05:44,799 --> 00:05:47,787
Let's say your investment earns seven percent

183
00:05:47,787 --> 00:05:48,583
before fees.

184
00:05:48,763 --> 00:05:50,766
In a low cost index fund with

185
00:05:50,766 --> 00:05:52,699
a fee of a small fraction of

186
00:05:52,699 --> 00:05:54,978
a percent, you keep almost all of

187
00:05:54,978 --> 00:05:55,323
that.

188
00:05:55,503 --> 00:05:57,600
In a fund charging one percent a

189
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:00,417
year, your effective return drops to around

190
00:06:00,417 --> 00:06:01,203
six percent.

191
00:06:01,383 --> 00:06:03,390
On one hundred dollars a month over

192
00:06:03,390 --> 00:06:06,313
twenty years, that's the difference between roughly

193
00:06:06,313 --> 00:06:08,835
fifty two thousand dollars and roughly forty

194
00:06:08,835 --> 00:06:09,580
six thousand.

195
00:06:09,931 --> 00:06:11,177
Over forty years,

196
00:06:11,177 --> 00:06:13,011
the gap gets much bigger,

197
00:06:13,011 --> 00:06:15,944
because fees compound just like returns.

198
00:06:16,124 --> 00:06:17,882
A one percent fee can take a

199
00:06:17,882 --> 00:06:20,771
surprisingly large share of your final balance

200
00:06:20,771 --> 00:06:22,591
over a lifetime of investing.

201
00:06:22,771 --> 00:06:24,842
And the fee is charged every year,

202
00:06:24,842 --> 00:06:27,218
whether your investments go up or down.

203
00:06:27,568 --> 00:06:29,690
This is a big reason low cost

204
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:31,886
index funds became so popular.

205
00:06:32,066 --> 00:06:34,395
Researchers like those at S and P

206
00:06:34,395 --> 00:06:35,665
Dow Jones Indices,

207
00:06:35,665 --> 00:06:37,287
in their SPIVA reports,

208
00:06:37,287 --> 00:06:39,968
have found that over long periods most

209
00:06:39,968 --> 00:06:44,131
actively managed funds underperformed their benchmark index

210
00:06:44,131 --> 00:06:44,907
after fees.

211
00:06:45,087 --> 00:06:46,984
Lower costs are one of the few

212
00:06:46,984 --> 00:06:49,197
things investors can fully control.

213
00:06:49,547 --> 00:06:50,243
Try this.

214
00:06:50,423 --> 00:06:52,933
Look up the expense ratio of every

215
00:06:52,933 --> 00:06:53,893
fund you own,

216
00:06:53,893 --> 00:06:56,994
in your retirement plan and anywhere else.

217
00:06:57,174 --> 00:06:59,216
If you see one percent or more,

218
00:06:59,216 --> 00:07:01,719
check whether your plan offers a lower

219
00:07:01,719 --> 00:07:04,222
cost alternative in the same category.

220
00:07:04,572 --> 00:07:05,709
Lesson number four.

221
00:07:05,889 --> 00:07:08,745
Inflation means future dollars buy less.

222
00:07:09,096 --> 00:07:12,006
Inflation is the gradual rise in prices

223
00:07:12,006 --> 00:07:12,753
over time.

224
00:07:12,933 --> 00:07:15,948
The Federal Reserve targets about two percent

225
00:07:15,948 --> 00:07:18,428
inflation a year over the longer run,

226
00:07:18,428 --> 00:07:21,376
and actual inflation has sometimes been much

227
00:07:21,376 --> 00:07:24,124
higher, as Americans saw in twenty twenty

228
00:07:24,124 --> 00:07:24,392
two.

229
00:07:24,572 --> 00:07:26,310
At three percent inflation,

230
00:07:26,310 --> 00:07:29,015
prices roughly double in about twenty four

231
00:07:29,015 --> 00:07:29,401
years.

232
00:07:29,752 --> 00:07:32,330
That's why the seven percent example is

233
00:07:32,330 --> 00:07:32,793
useful.

234
00:07:32,973 --> 00:07:35,727
Because it's roughly a return after inflation,

235
00:07:35,727 --> 00:07:38,241
fifty two thousand dollars in twenty years

236
00:07:38,241 --> 00:07:40,575
means roughly the buying power of fifty

237
00:07:40,575 --> 00:07:42,192
two thousand dollars today.

238
00:07:42,372 --> 00:07:44,365
If you used a ten percent nominal

239
00:07:44,365 --> 00:07:46,418
return, you'd get a bigger number,

240
00:07:46,418 --> 00:07:47,747
but it would buy less.

241
00:07:48,097 --> 00:07:50,694
Inflation is also why keeping long term

242
00:07:50,694 --> 00:07:52,626
money in cash is risky in its

243
00:07:52,626 --> 00:07:53,158
own way.

244
00:07:53,338 --> 00:07:56,311
A savings account paying less than inflation

245
00:07:56,311 --> 00:07:58,067
slowly loses buying power,

246
00:07:58,067 --> 00:08:00,769
even though the balance never goes down.

247
00:08:00,949 --> 00:08:02,316
For short term money,

248
00:08:02,316 --> 00:08:03,097
that's fine.

249
00:08:03,277 --> 00:08:04,835
For twenty year money,

250
00:08:04,835 --> 00:08:06,040
it's a real cost.

251
00:08:06,390 --> 00:08:07,249
Try this.

252
00:08:07,429 --> 00:08:09,837
When you see any long term projection,

253
00:08:09,837 --> 00:08:11,993
ask, is this in today's dollars or

254
00:08:11,993 --> 00:08:12,943
future dollars?

255
00:08:13,123 --> 00:08:15,354
It's one of the most useful questions

256
00:08:15,354 --> 00:08:16,560
in personal finance.

257
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:18,117
Lesson number five.

258
00:08:18,297 --> 00:08:20,050
The type of account changes what you

259
00:08:20,050 --> 00:08:20,294
keep.

260
00:08:20,644 --> 00:08:23,138
In a regular taxable brokerage account,

261
00:08:23,138 --> 00:08:25,377
you may owe taxes on dividends each

262
00:08:25,377 --> 00:08:27,424
year and on gains when you sell.

263
00:08:27,604 --> 00:08:30,496
In tax advantaged accounts like a four

264
00:08:30,496 --> 00:08:31,943
oh one k or an IRA,

265
00:08:31,943 --> 00:08:34,988
the money can grow without yearly taxes.

266
00:08:35,168 --> 00:08:37,252
That difference can add up to thousands

267
00:08:37,252 --> 00:08:38,802
of dollars over twenty years.

268
00:08:39,152 --> 00:08:42,405
A Roth IRA is especially powerful for

269
00:08:42,405 --> 00:08:45,131
small, long term contributions.

270
00:08:45,311 --> 00:08:48,256
You contribute money you've already paid tax

271
00:08:48,256 --> 00:08:50,399
on, and if you follow the rules,

272
00:08:50,399 --> 00:08:53,680
qualified withdrawals in retirement are tax free,

273
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:55,353
including all the growth.

274
00:08:55,533 --> 00:08:58,140
On our fifty two thousand dollar example,

275
00:08:58,140 --> 00:09:00,747
that could mean all twenty eight thousand

276
00:09:00,747 --> 00:09:02,337
of growth is never taxed.

277
00:09:02,517 --> 00:09:04,802
Income limits and rules apply,

278
00:09:04,802 --> 00:09:07,544
so check the current IRS guidelines.

279
00:09:07,894 --> 00:09:10,352
A traditional four oh one k or

280
00:09:10,352 --> 00:09:12,154
IRA works differently.

281
00:09:12,334 --> 00:09:15,341
Contributions may reduce your taxes today,

282
00:09:15,341 --> 00:09:17,561
and you pay income tax when you

283
00:09:17,561 --> 00:09:19,207
withdraw in retirement.

284
00:09:19,387 --> 00:09:20,908
Both can be great choices.

285
00:09:21,088 --> 00:09:23,008
The best one depends on your tax

286
00:09:23,008 --> 00:09:25,047
bracket now and what you expect it

287
00:09:25,047 --> 00:09:25,767
to be later.

288
00:09:26,118 --> 00:09:26,942
Try this.

289
00:09:27,122 --> 00:09:29,560
If you're investing one hundred dollars a

290
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:31,880
month, find out whether you have access

291
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:33,842
to a workplace plan with a match,

292
00:09:33,842 --> 00:09:36,102
and whether you're eligible for a Roth

293
00:09:36,102 --> 00:09:36,340
IRA.

294
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:38,760
Those are usually the first places to

295
00:09:38,760 --> 00:09:39,063
look.

296
00:09:39,413 --> 00:09:40,852
Lesson number six.

297
00:09:41,032 --> 00:09:42,508
Increasing the amount,

298
00:09:42,508 --> 00:09:44,654
even slowly, changes everything.

299
00:09:45,005 --> 00:09:47,193
One hundred dollars a month for twenty

300
00:09:47,193 --> 00:09:49,728
years at seven percent becomes roughly fifty

301
00:09:49,728 --> 00:09:50,937
two thousand dollars.

302
00:09:51,117 --> 00:09:53,550
Two hundred dollars becomes roughly one hundred

303
00:09:53,550 --> 00:09:54,275
four thousand.

304
00:09:54,455 --> 00:09:57,188
Five hundred dollars becomes roughly two hundred

305
00:09:57,188 --> 00:09:58,043
sixty thousand.

306
00:09:58,223 --> 00:10:00,556
The growth scales directly with the amount.

307
00:10:00,907 --> 00:10:02,500
But you don't need to jump straight

308
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:03,229
to five hundred.

309
00:10:03,409 --> 00:10:05,222
If you start at one hundred and

310
00:10:05,222 --> 00:10:06,977
raise it by just a few percent

311
00:10:06,977 --> 00:10:08,966
every year, perhaps when you get a

312
00:10:08,966 --> 00:10:11,481
raise, the final result can be dramatically

313
00:10:11,481 --> 00:10:13,997
higher than staying at one hundred forever.

314
00:10:14,177 --> 00:10:17,741
Small increases compound on top of compounding.

315
00:10:18,091 --> 00:10:20,721
This is where many people's financial lives

316
00:10:20,721 --> 00:10:21,945
quietly turn around.

317
00:10:22,125 --> 00:10:23,286
They start small,

318
00:10:23,286 --> 00:10:24,721
see the balance grow,

319
00:10:24,721 --> 00:10:27,384
feel motivated, and gradually increase.

320
00:10:27,564 --> 00:10:28,642
Five years later,

321
00:10:28,642 --> 00:10:31,811
they're investing several hundred dollars a month,

322
00:10:31,811 --> 00:10:33,079
and it feels normal.

323
00:10:33,429 --> 00:10:34,288
Try this.

324
00:10:34,468 --> 00:10:36,833
Set a calendar reminder for every raise

325
00:10:36,833 --> 00:10:37,925
or every new year,

326
00:10:37,925 --> 00:10:40,412
to increase your monthly investment by at

327
00:10:40,412 --> 00:10:41,503
least ten dollars.

328
00:10:41,683 --> 00:10:43,972
You'll barely notice the difference in your

329
00:10:43,972 --> 00:10:45,782
budget, but your future self will.

330
00:10:46,132 --> 00:10:48,480
Here's what five hundred dollars a month

331
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:50,593
becomes over twenty years at a seven

332
00:10:50,593 --> 00:10:52,354
percent average yearly return.

333
00:10:52,534 --> 00:10:54,926
Roughly two hundred sixty thousand dollars.

334
00:10:55,106 --> 00:10:56,085
Total put in,

335
00:10:56,085 --> 00:10:58,194
one hundred twenty thousand.

336
00:10:58,545 --> 00:10:59,787
Lesson number seven.

337
00:10:59,967 --> 00:11:01,441
The path won't be smooth.

338
00:11:01,792 --> 00:11:03,870
Seven percent is an average.

339
00:11:04,050 --> 00:11:04,917
In real life,

340
00:11:04,917 --> 00:11:07,185
some years will be much better and

341
00:11:07,185 --> 00:11:08,253
some much worse.

342
00:11:08,433 --> 00:11:10,838
The US stock market fell about thirty

343
00:11:10,838 --> 00:11:13,178
seven percent in two thousand eight,

344
00:11:13,178 --> 00:11:16,428
including dividends, and about eighteen percent in

345
00:11:16,428 --> 00:11:17,598
twenty twenty two,

346
00:11:17,598 --> 00:11:19,743
according to S and P five hundred

347
00:11:19,743 --> 00:11:20,913
total return data.

348
00:11:21,093 --> 00:11:22,773
There will be years when your one

349
00:11:22,773 --> 00:11:24,555
hundred dollars a month seems to be

350
00:11:24,555 --> 00:11:25,064
shrinking.

351
00:11:25,414 --> 00:11:26,633
But here's the twist.

352
00:11:26,813 --> 00:11:29,111
When you invest the same amount every

353
00:11:29,111 --> 00:11:31,223
month, bad years mean you buy more

354
00:11:31,223 --> 00:11:32,652
shares at lower prices.

355
00:11:32,832 --> 00:11:35,073
That's called dollar cost averaging.

356
00:11:35,253 --> 00:11:38,380
It doesn't guarantee profits or prevent losses,

357
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:40,842
but it means market drops aren't only

358
00:11:40,842 --> 00:11:41,441
bad news.

359
00:11:41,621 --> 00:11:43,287
They're also a sale on what you're

360
00:11:43,287 --> 00:11:43,630
buying.

361
00:11:43,979 --> 00:11:47,578
Historically, investors who kept investing through downturns

362
00:11:47,578 --> 00:11:49,976
were generally better off than those who

363
00:11:49,976 --> 00:11:51,596
stopped or sold in a panic.

364
00:11:51,776 --> 00:11:53,982
The biggest risk for a small monthly

365
00:11:53,982 --> 00:11:55,944
investor often isn't the market.

366
00:11:56,124 --> 00:11:56,936
It's quitting.

367
00:11:57,286 --> 00:11:58,076
Try this.

368
00:11:58,256 --> 00:12:00,134
Write a one sentence note to your

369
00:12:00,134 --> 00:12:01,158
future self today.

370
00:12:01,338 --> 00:12:02,533
When the market drops,

371
00:12:02,533 --> 00:12:04,487
I will keep investing my one hundred

372
00:12:04,487 --> 00:12:05,356
dollars a month.

373
00:12:05,536 --> 00:12:06,964
Put it where you'll see it.

374
00:12:07,313 --> 00:12:08,265
That's angle one.

375
00:12:08,445 --> 00:12:10,054
Time, return rate,

376
00:12:10,054 --> 00:12:12,109
fees, inflation, taxes,

377
00:12:12,109 --> 00:12:13,807
raising the amount,

378
00:12:13,807 --> 00:12:15,504
and the bumpy path.

379
00:12:15,684 --> 00:12:16,996
Now angle two.

380
00:12:17,176 --> 00:12:18,360
The psychology.

381
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:20,189
Because the math is simple.

382
00:12:20,369 --> 00:12:22,692
The hard part is actually doing it,

383
00:12:22,692 --> 00:12:23,887
month after month.

384
00:12:24,236 --> 00:12:25,725
If the math is so clear,

385
00:12:25,725 --> 00:12:28,640
why doesn't everyone invest one hundred dollars

386
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:29,136
a month?

387
00:12:29,316 --> 00:12:31,687
Because our brains aren't built for long

388
00:12:31,687 --> 00:12:32,694
term compounding.

389
00:12:32,874 --> 00:12:34,653
We think in straight lines,

390
00:12:34,653 --> 00:12:36,563
we value today over tomorrow,

391
00:12:36,563 --> 00:12:38,737
and small amounts feel pointless.

392
00:12:38,917 --> 00:12:40,619
Let's look at the mental hurdles,

393
00:12:40,619 --> 00:12:41,959
and how to jump over them.

394
00:12:42,309 --> 00:12:43,505
Lesson number eight.

395
00:12:43,685 --> 00:12:45,989
One hundred dollars feels too small to

396
00:12:45,989 --> 00:12:46,414
bother.

397
00:12:46,763 --> 00:12:47,855
Many people think,

398
00:12:47,855 --> 00:12:50,342
what's the point of investing one hundred

399
00:12:50,342 --> 00:12:50,827
dollars?

400
00:12:51,007 --> 00:12:52,795
I'll start when I can invest real

401
00:12:52,795 --> 00:12:53,120
money.

402
00:12:53,300 --> 00:12:54,885
But waiting costs time,

403
00:12:54,885 --> 00:12:57,712
and time is the most valuable ingredient.

404
00:12:57,892 --> 00:13:00,306
Five years of waiting can mean losing

405
00:13:00,306 --> 00:13:02,524
a large share of the final result.

406
00:13:02,875 --> 00:13:06,782
Economists Victor Stango and Jonathan Zinman described

407
00:13:06,782 --> 00:13:08,519
exponential growth bias,

408
00:13:08,519 --> 00:13:11,559
our tendency to underestimate compounding.

409
00:13:11,739 --> 00:13:14,416
We imagine growth as a straight line,

410
00:13:14,416 --> 00:13:17,021
so small amounts look small forever.

411
00:13:17,201 --> 00:13:20,034
In reality, they curve upward over time.

412
00:13:20,385 --> 00:13:22,451
There's also a habit benefit.

413
00:13:22,631 --> 00:13:25,497
Investing one hundred dollars teaches you how

414
00:13:25,497 --> 00:13:27,535
accounts work, how markets move,

415
00:13:27,535 --> 00:13:29,128
and how it feels to see a

416
00:13:29,128 --> 00:13:29,446
drop.

417
00:13:29,626 --> 00:13:31,257
When you have more money later,

418
00:13:31,257 --> 00:13:32,889
you'll already know what to do.

419
00:13:33,239 --> 00:13:34,028
Try this.

420
00:13:34,208 --> 00:13:36,984
Instead of asking whether one hundred dollars

421
00:13:36,984 --> 00:13:39,328
matters, ask what it becomes in twenty

422
00:13:39,328 --> 00:13:40,315
or thirty years.

423
00:13:40,495 --> 00:13:41,784
Then start this month.

424
00:13:42,134 --> 00:13:43,353
Lesson number nine.

425
00:13:43,533 --> 00:13:45,228
There's no perfect time to start.

426
00:13:45,578 --> 00:13:47,939
People often wait for the market to

427
00:13:47,939 --> 00:13:50,166
drop, or for things to calm down,

428
00:13:50,166 --> 00:13:51,313
before investing.

429
00:13:51,493 --> 00:13:54,286
But nobody can reliably predict short term

430
00:13:54,286 --> 00:13:55,150
market moves.

431
00:13:55,330 --> 00:13:57,624
Missing even a handful of the best

432
00:13:57,624 --> 00:14:00,593
days in the market historically reduced long

433
00:14:00,593 --> 00:14:02,414
term returns significantly,

434
00:14:02,414 --> 00:14:04,978
according to analyses by firms like J.

435
00:14:05,158 --> 00:14:05,390
P.

436
00:14:05,570 --> 00:14:07,265
Morgan Asset Management.

437
00:14:07,616 --> 00:14:09,960
Many of the best days happened close

438
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:11,133
to the worst days,

439
00:14:11,133 --> 00:14:12,956
during periods of high fear.

440
00:14:13,136 --> 00:14:15,315
Investors who sat out to avoid the

441
00:14:15,315 --> 00:14:17,815
bad days often missed the recovery too.

442
00:14:17,995 --> 00:14:18,862
In other words,

443
00:14:18,862 --> 00:14:21,176
the moments that feel most dangerous are

444
00:14:21,176 --> 00:14:23,894
often the moments when staying invested matters

445
00:14:23,894 --> 00:14:24,183
most.

446
00:14:24,533 --> 00:14:26,001
For a monthly investor,

447
00:14:26,001 --> 00:14:27,470
the solution is simple.

448
00:14:27,650 --> 00:14:29,901
Invest the same amount on the same

449
00:14:29,901 --> 00:14:30,960
day every month,

450
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:32,549
regardless of headlines.

451
00:14:32,729 --> 00:14:35,016
That removes the timing decision entirely.

452
00:14:35,367 --> 00:14:36,063
Try this.

453
00:14:36,243 --> 00:14:38,068
Pick a date right after payday and

454
00:14:38,068 --> 00:14:40,376
set your automatic investment for that day.

455
00:14:40,556 --> 00:14:42,809
Then stop checking the news to decide.

456
00:14:43,159 --> 00:14:44,366
Lesson number ten.

457
00:14:44,546 --> 00:14:47,245
Today always feels more important than twenty

458
00:14:47,245 --> 00:14:48,145
years from now.

459
00:14:48,495 --> 00:14:51,523
Present bias makes immediate rewards feel much

460
00:14:51,523 --> 00:14:53,104
bigger than future ones.

461
00:14:53,284 --> 00:14:55,269
One hundred dollars today feels like a

462
00:14:55,269 --> 00:14:55,896
nice dinner.

463
00:14:56,076 --> 00:14:59,002
Fifty two thousand dollars in twenty years

464
00:14:59,002 --> 00:15:00,047
feels abstract.

465
00:15:00,397 --> 00:15:02,792
Some research has found that when people

466
00:15:02,792 --> 00:15:05,187
see age progressed images of themselves,

467
00:15:05,187 --> 00:15:07,342
they become more willing to save for

468
00:15:07,342 --> 00:15:08,001
the future.

469
00:15:08,181 --> 00:15:10,689
Making the future feel real helps fight

470
00:15:10,689 --> 00:15:11,525
present bias.

471
00:15:11,875 --> 00:15:13,823
Another trick is to tie the future

472
00:15:13,823 --> 00:15:15,427
money to something specific.

473
00:15:15,607 --> 00:15:16,948
Not just retirement,

474
00:15:16,948 --> 00:15:18,958
but freedom to change careers,

475
00:15:18,958 --> 00:15:20,098
a paid off house,

476
00:15:20,098 --> 00:15:21,505
or helping your kids.

477
00:15:21,685 --> 00:15:24,065
Specific goals feel more real.

478
00:15:24,415 --> 00:15:25,181
Try this.

479
00:15:25,361 --> 00:15:27,984
Give your investment account a nickname,

480
00:15:27,984 --> 00:15:30,214
like freedom fund or future house.

481
00:15:30,394 --> 00:15:31,391
It sounds small,

482
00:15:31,391 --> 00:15:33,385
but naming your goal can make it

483
00:15:33,385 --> 00:15:34,132
feel closer.

484
00:15:34,483 --> 00:15:35,818
Lesson number eleven.

485
00:15:35,998 --> 00:15:38,076
Checking your account too often can make

486
00:15:38,076 --> 00:15:39,167
you a worse investor.

487
00:15:39,518 --> 00:15:41,677
Markets go up and down daily.

488
00:15:41,857 --> 00:15:43,169
If you check every day,

489
00:15:43,169 --> 00:15:44,539
you'll see a lot of red.

490
00:15:44,719 --> 00:15:47,759
Research on loss aversion shows losses feel

491
00:15:47,759 --> 00:15:50,374
about twice as painful as equal gains

492
00:15:50,374 --> 00:15:51,081
feel good.

493
00:15:51,261 --> 00:15:53,583
Frequent checking magnifies the pain.

494
00:15:53,934 --> 00:15:57,281
Studies by Shlomo Benartzi and Richard Thaler

495
00:15:57,281 --> 00:15:59,588
described myopic loss aversion.

496
00:15:59,768 --> 00:16:02,393
Investors who looked at results more often

497
00:16:02,393 --> 00:16:04,581
tended to take less risk and choose

498
00:16:04,581 --> 00:16:06,269
worse long term strategies.

499
00:16:06,620 --> 00:16:08,187
For a twenty year plan,

500
00:16:08,187 --> 00:16:10,504
checking once a month or even once

501
00:16:10,504 --> 00:16:11,867
a quarter is plenty.

502
00:16:12,047 --> 00:16:14,137
You'll see the long term trend instead

503
00:16:14,137 --> 00:16:15,182
of the daily noise.

504
00:16:15,532 --> 00:16:16,391
Try this.

505
00:16:16,571 --> 00:16:19,018
Delete the investing app from your home

506
00:16:19,018 --> 00:16:21,528
screen, or turn off price notifications.

507
00:16:21,708 --> 00:16:23,321
Let the money work while you live

508
00:16:23,321 --> 00:16:23,810
your life.

509
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:24,961
Quick break.

510
00:16:25,141 --> 00:16:27,159
If this is helping you see small

511
00:16:27,159 --> 00:16:30,249
money differently, subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

512
00:16:30,429 --> 00:16:32,271
It's free and it helps the channel

513
00:16:32,271 --> 00:16:32,542
grow.

514
00:16:32,722 --> 00:16:34,510
Okay, lesson twelve.

515
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:36,241
Lesson number twelve.

516
00:16:36,421 --> 00:16:39,047
Don't compare your hundred dollars with someone

517
00:16:39,047 --> 00:16:39,997
else's thousands.

518
00:16:40,347 --> 00:16:43,800
Online, you'll see people investing thousands a

519
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:45,931
month, or showing huge gains.

520
00:16:46,111 --> 00:16:48,057
That can make one hundred dollars feel

521
00:16:48,057 --> 00:16:48,723
embarrassing.

522
00:16:48,903 --> 00:16:50,645
But comparison is a trap.

523
00:16:50,825 --> 00:16:52,288
You don't know their income,

524
00:16:52,288 --> 00:16:53,750
their debt, or their losses.

525
00:16:54,101 --> 00:16:56,156
Every investor starts somewhere.

526
00:16:56,336 --> 00:16:59,210
Many people who invest large amounts today

527
00:16:59,210 --> 00:17:02,837
started with small automatic contributions years ago.

528
00:17:03,017 --> 00:17:04,921
The difference is that they kept going.

529
00:17:05,272 --> 00:17:07,116
Your plan only has to work for

530
00:17:07,116 --> 00:17:07,361
you.

531
00:17:07,541 --> 00:17:09,604
One hundred dollars a month,

532
00:17:09,604 --> 00:17:12,403
kept up for decades and increased over

533
00:17:12,403 --> 00:17:13,950
time, is a real plan.

534
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:15,147
Try this.

535
00:17:15,327 --> 00:17:17,301
Track only your own progress.

536
00:17:17,481 --> 00:17:19,889
Write down your balance every six months

537
00:17:19,889 --> 00:17:21,997
and compare it with your past self.

538
00:17:22,347 --> 00:17:23,844
Lesson number thirteen.

539
00:17:24,024 --> 00:17:26,468
Fear of losing money keeps many people

540
00:17:26,468 --> 00:17:27,368
from starting.

541
00:17:27,718 --> 00:17:29,587
Investing does involve risk.

542
00:17:29,767 --> 00:17:31,541
Stock prices can fall,

543
00:17:31,541 --> 00:17:32,832
sometimes a lot.

544
00:17:33,012 --> 00:17:35,171
But not investing has its own risk.

545
00:17:35,351 --> 00:17:37,225
Inflation slowly erodes cash,

546
00:17:37,225 --> 00:17:39,745
and missing decades of growth can leave

547
00:17:39,745 --> 00:17:41,296
you short in retirement.

548
00:17:41,646 --> 00:17:44,764
Diversification reduces the risk of any single

549
00:17:44,764 --> 00:17:45,848
company failing.

550
00:17:46,028 --> 00:17:48,780
Broad index funds hold hundreds or thousands

551
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:49,593
of companies.

552
00:17:49,773 --> 00:17:52,691
A long time horizon gives investments time

553
00:17:52,691 --> 00:17:54,498
to recover from downturns.

554
00:17:54,678 --> 00:17:57,572
Historically, the longer the holding period,

555
00:17:57,572 --> 00:18:00,268
the less often broad US stock investments

556
00:18:00,268 --> 00:18:01,452
ended with a loss,

557
00:18:01,452 --> 00:18:04,017
though there are no guarantees and some

558
00:18:04,017 --> 00:18:06,450
long periods have been disappointing.

559
00:18:06,801 --> 00:18:09,239
Your emergency fund also helps.

560
00:18:09,419 --> 00:18:11,218
If you have cash for surprises,

561
00:18:11,218 --> 00:18:13,482
you won't be forced to sell investments

562
00:18:13,482 --> 00:18:14,295
at a bad time.

563
00:18:14,645 --> 00:18:15,504
Try this.

564
00:18:15,684 --> 00:18:19,190
Before investing, build a small emergency fund.

565
00:18:19,370 --> 00:18:21,527
Then invest money you won't need for

566
00:18:21,527 --> 00:18:22,725
at least five years.

567
00:18:23,076 --> 00:18:24,597
Lesson number fourteen.

568
00:18:24,777 --> 00:18:27,551
Motivation fades, so systems matter.

569
00:18:27,902 --> 00:18:29,364
Starting is exciting.

570
00:18:29,544 --> 00:18:31,414
Month thirty seven is boring.

571
00:18:31,594 --> 00:18:34,814
Many people stop contributing after the novelty

572
00:18:34,814 --> 00:18:37,143
wears off, or when life gets busy.

573
00:18:37,323 --> 00:18:38,860
A move, a new baby,

574
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:39,911
a job change,

575
00:18:39,911 --> 00:18:43,227
and suddenly the transfer gets paused and

